_rl_agent.backend_execution_environment_verification.v1","status":"pass"},"candidate":{"candidate":{"candidate_id":"push_recovery_lateral_tilt_decay_v1","entrypoint":"training_code.components:build_training_components","schema_version":"true_rl_agent.candidate.v1"},"components":{"effective_hook_counts":{"diagnostics":0,"reward_terms":7,"schedules":0},"is_noop":false,"metadata":{},"name":"push_standing_recovery_tilt_decay_v1","rationale":"Closest prior program: the accepted current best push_recovery_lateral_brace_v1 (public_dev mean 0.9115; its parent push_recovery_nonsaturating_stance_v1 was 0.8802). Its public_dev breakdown is 175 success_recovered, 10 fall_side and 7 not_recovered: after the lateral brace landed, height_collapse and fall_forward vanished completely, so every residual toppling failure is a sideways roll collapse, concentrated in the high (37/48) and repeated (42/48) strata. Negative evidence that is not repeated: the sibling push_recovery_side_braced_stance_v1 (0.8385, height_collapse dominated) and the earlier push_recovery_tilt_aware_stance_v2 and push_recovery_vel_damped_stance_v1 all strengthened level or speed penalties in the displacement regime that contains the recovery transient. Sole mechanism change: one new RewardTerm lateral_tilt_decay is added; the anchor's upright_recovery kernel, lateral_brace, stationary_v3, joint_pose, action_rate and fall callables, their weights, signs, clips, term order, and the empty schedule and diagnostic lists are preserved exactly. lateral_tilt_decay computes roll_rate = base_ang_vel_scaled[:, 0], the morphology-scaled world-frame roll rate, and level_gate = clamp((abs(roll) - 0.10) / 0.10, 0, 1), which reuses the accepted brace's 0.10 rad roll deadband, and returns -(level_gate * clamp(roll * roll_rate, 0, inf)). It is the one-sided derivative of the lateral tilt energy: it charges a state only while the sideways tilt magnitude is growing, it is exactly zero while the base sits inside the nominal stance band, and it is exactly zero whenever roll * roll_rate is negative, that is while the base is already rolling back toward level. The anchor prices the lateral state with three level quantities, namely the squared tilt branch inside upright_recovery, the linear roll brace, and the symmetric lateral speed term; none of them separates a still-growing topple from an already-decaying one, so at the tipping onset the only lateral gradient available scales with the current roll rather than with the diverging rate that decides whether the fresh stable dwell is reached before the base passes the point of no return. The new term supplies exactly that missing one-sided rate gradient, which shortens the window in which a repeated-pulse sequence can re-topple a base that is still rolling, and it touches no translational channel, so it cannot brake the lateral stepping strategy that the accepted speed terms already bound, which is the failure mode of the refuted speed-damping route. It is also structurally distinct from the excluded single-component card RewardTerm:roll_brace, which depends on roll alone as a level brace: this mechanism pairs roll with the world-frame roll rate and is one-sided in the growth direction, with clip [-1.0, 0.0] at weight -1.5 bounding the added contribution to 1.5, the same order as the accepted lateral_brace, so it shapes the tipping onset rather than competing with the -20 fall penalty. The success definition, the pulse schedule, and all task-owned, evaluation-owned, checkpoint-owned and budget-owned surfaces are untouched.","terms":{"diagnostics":[],"reward_terms":[{"clip_max":1.0,"clip_min":-4.0,"description":"Deadbanded non-saturating stance potential over relative base height and tilt.","kind":"RewardTerm","name":"upright_recovery","weight":3.0},{"clip_max":0.0,"clip_min":-2.0,"description":"Penalty on sideways tilt beyond a deadband and lateral base speed.","kind":"RewardTerm","name":"lateral_brace","weight":-1.0},{"clip_max":0.0,"clip_min":-1